    "lawCode": "Amd §502, V & T L; amd §14, Transp L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5486\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law and the transportation law,\nin relation to establishing a commercial driver's license (CDL) class A\nyoung adult licensing pilot program\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo create a young adult commercial driver's license (CDL) Class A\nlicensing pilot program.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Paragraphs (a) and (b) of subdivision 2 of section 502 of the\nvehicles and traffic law.\n\nSection 2: Amends section 14 of the transportation law to add a new\nsubdivision 36 to create rules for the pilot program. This establishes\nwho can supervise young adult drivers, requirements for the program, and\nfuture reporting of the program results.\n\nSection 3: Repeals act if any federal agency determines that the act\nwould render NYS ineligible for federal funds.\n\nSection 4: Severability.\n\nSection 5: Establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York's Commercial Drivers are the backbone of the state's economy.\nThe American Transportation Research Institute claims the trucking\nindustry is responsible for more than 275,000 jobs, or 1 out of 27 in\nthe state, and pays wages in excess of $13.6 billion annually.  s.k\nHowever, New York's trucking industry is finding it increasingly diffi-\ncult to recruit commercial drivers to supply the state's 38,580 trucking\ncompanies, most of them small, locally owned businesses. An analysis\ncommissioned by the American Trucking Association concluded that as of\nthe end of 2017 the industry had a 50,000-driver shortage.  With the\nindustry estimating it needs to hire 890,000 drivers over the next 10\nyears.\n\nCurrently New York allows 18 to 20-year old's to obtain a CDL Class B\nlicense to drive within state lines but does not allow those same driv-\ners to obtain a CDL Class A. Class A CDL licenses allow drivers to oper-\nate tractor-trailers and other vehicles that are too large for a Class B\nCDL. A young adult commercial driver's license pilot program, with\nstrict training and safety guidelines, would allow 18 to 20-year-olds to\ndrive in New York could help attract more young people into the field.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nFormerly A-10140 of 2020, died in the Transportation Committee.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nThis legislation has no fiscal implications.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effective immediately\n",
