    "lawCode": "Amd §502, V & T L; amd §14, Transp L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5486A\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law and the transportation law,\nin relation to establishing a commercial driver's license (CDL) class A\nyoung adult training program\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo create a young adult commercial driver's license (CDL) Class A\nlicensing program.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Paragraphs (a) and (b) of subdivision 2 of section 502 of the\nvehicles and traffic law.\n\nSection 2: Subdivision 5 of section 502 of the vehicle and traffic law\nis amended by adding a new paragraph (d). This establishes the completed\nrequirement of a CDL class A young training program before licensing.\n\nSection 3: Section 144 of the transportation law is amended by adding a\nnew Subdivision 36.\n\nSection 4: This section would repeal this act if NY became ineligible\nfor the receipt of federal funds.\n\nSection 5: Severability.\n\nSection 6: Establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York's Commercial Drivers are the backbone of the state's economy.\nThe American Transportation Research Institute claims the trucking\nindustry is responsible for more than 275,000 jobs, or 1 out of 27 in\nthe state, and pays wages in excess of $13.6 billion annually.  However,\nNew York's trucking industry is finding it increasingly difficult to\nrecruit commercial drivers to supply the state's 38,580 trucking compa-\nnies, most of them small, locally owned businesses. An analysis commis-\nsioned by the American Trucking Association concluded that as of the end\nof 2017 the industry had a 50,000-driver shortage.  With the industry\nestimating it needs to hire 890,000 drivers over the next 10 years.\n\nCurrently New York allows 18 to 20-year old's to obtain a CDL Class B\nlicense to drive within state lines but does not allow those same driv-\ners to obtain a CDL Class A. Class A CDL licenses allow drivers to oper-\nate tractor-trailers and other vehicles that are too large for a Class B\nCDL. A young adult commercial driver's license training program, with\nstrict training and safety guidelines, would allow 18 to 20-year-olds to\ndrive in New York could help attract more young people into the field.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nFormerly S.5486/A-10140 of 2020, died in the Transportation Committee.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\nThis legislation has no fiscal implications.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred eightieth day.\n",
